# Diagonal lemma

The **diagonal lemma**, also called the fixed-point lemma, states that in any
theory able to represent its own syntax, every property of formulas has a
sentence that provably asserts the property of itself. It is the exact
statement behind the phrase "a sentence that talks about itself", and the
step that closes the loop opened by [[godel-numbering]].

## Description

Let $T$ be a theory extending Robinson arithmetic $Q$, with a Gödel numbering
of its formulas. For every formula $\varphi(x)$ with one free variable there
is a sentence $G$ such that

$$T\vdash G\leftrightarrow\varphi(\ulcorner G\urcorner).$$

Three things about the statement matter for the discussion.

- **The self-reference is indirect.** $G$ does not contain itself. It contains
  a description from which its own code can be computed, and the theory proves
  the equivalence. "Refers to itself" is shorthand for a provable
  biconditional.
- **It is a lemma about existence, with no consequence yet.** It holds for
  every $\varphi$. Choosing $\varphi$ as "is a formula with fewer than a
  thousand symbols" gives a self-describing sentence that is merely true or
  false. The discussion separated the *existence of self-reference* from *a
  theorem about its consequences*: the lemma is the first, and the
  [[incompleteness-theorems]] are the second.
- **It needs no consistency assumption.** The lemma holds in inconsistent
  theories too, trivially. Consistency enters only when a particular fixed
  point is examined.

The proof is a [[diagonalization]]. Define the function that takes the code of
a formula $\alpha(x)$ to the code of $\alpha(\ulcorner\alpha\urcorner)$, the
formula applied to its own code. This function is computable, so it is
representable in $T$. Let $\beta(x)$ say "the diagonalization of $x$ has
property $\varphi$". Applying $\beta$ to its own code gives $G$. The table of
all formulas against all codes is being read down its diagonal, as in Cantor's
argument, but here the diagonal entry is fed back in rather than negated.

The choice that produces the celebrated case is
$\varphi(x)=\neg\operatorname{Prov}_T(x)$, which yields the
[[godel-sentence]]. Other choices give other classical results: the negation
of a supposed truth predicate gives Tarski's undefinability of truth, and
$\operatorname{Prov}_T(x)$ itself, unnegated, gives the sentence studied by
Löb.

Among the seven candidate definitions collected in this vault, the fourth, the
Gödelian one, is the diagonal lemma plus a consequence: a theory that supports
diagonal self-reference *and* whose fixed points have formal consequences such
as incompleteness.

## Relations

- It is a [[fixed-point]] {relation: instance-of} theorem: the sentence is fixed, up to provable equivalence, by the operation "assert the property of my code".
- Its proof is a [[diagonalization]] {relation: uses}, applied to formulas and their codes.
- It presupposes [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses}: without codes there is nothing for a formula to be applied to.
- Its best-known instance is the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: generalizes}.
- It is the lemma from which the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: part-of} are derived.
- It is the formal counterpart of [[self-reference]] {relation: example-of} in arithmetic.
- The same lemma gives [[tarski-undefinability]] {relation: generalizes} when applied to a truth predicate.
- Its computational twin is the [[kleene-recursion-theorem]] {relation: analogous-to}: programs in place of sentences.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}: it shows self-reference alone is cheap, and the strangeness lies in what follows from it.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[godel-sentence]].** The fixed point for "is not provable in $T$". It is
  the lemma with one particular property plugged in.
- **[[fixed-point]].** A value that an operation returns unchanged. The lemma
  says the operation of predicating something of one's own code always has
  one.
- **[[diagonalization]].** The method of applying an enumerated family to its
  own index. The lemma is its self-referential form, Cantor's argument its
  escaping form.
- **[[incompleteness-theorems]].** What follows when the lemma is applied to
  unprovability in a consistent theory: a sentence the theory cannot decide.

